1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,780
Okay, the last section of this chapter deals with the pleura.

2
00:00:03,660 --> 00:00:05,920
Let's remind ourselves that if this is the lung,

3
00:00:06,760 --> 00:00:10,680
there attached to the lung is something called the visceral pleura.

4
00:00:11,900 --> 00:00:14,960
And this then folds over, and when it folds over,

5
00:00:15,040 --> 00:00:18,320
it attaches to the chest wall, and here we call it the parietal pleura.

6
00:00:18,700 --> 00:00:21,180
So again, there's a parietal pleura and a visceral pleura.

7
00:00:21,540 --> 00:00:23,860
In between that is the pleural space.

8
00:00:24,260 --> 00:00:27,340
Now remember that both the parietal pleura and the visceral pleura

9
00:00:27,340 --> 00:00:29,440
are lined by mesothelial cells.

10
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:35,580
The mesothelial cells produce a fluid that helps to lubricate the pleural space

11
00:00:35,580 --> 00:00:39,520
to facilitate the movement of the lung against the chest wall.

12
00:00:40,200 --> 00:00:42,500
The first disorder is called a pneumothorax.

13
00:00:42,580 --> 00:00:45,840
This is where there's accumulation of air in the pleural space.

14
00:00:46,400 --> 00:00:49,580
One type of pneumothorax is a spontaneous pneumothorax.

15
00:00:49,580 --> 00:00:53,120
This is usually due to rupture of an emphyseminous bleb,

16
00:00:53,540 --> 00:00:55,000
classically seen in young adults.

17
00:00:55,400 --> 00:00:57,720
This results in collapse of a portion of the lung,

18
00:00:57,720 --> 00:01:00,860
with the trachea shifting to the side of the collapse.

19
00:01:01,260 --> 00:01:03,200
Here's a perfect picture that illustrates that.

20
00:01:03,440 --> 00:01:05,100
This is the normal lung on this side,

21
00:01:05,480 --> 00:01:07,540
and you can see that on this particular side,

22
00:01:07,620 --> 00:01:09,720
the lung has actually collapsed,

23
00:01:09,920 --> 00:01:14,720
and the thorax is full of air, hence the term pneumothorax.

24
00:01:14,720 --> 00:01:19,980
Now, a tension pneumothorax arises with a penetrating chest wall injury,

25
00:01:20,440 --> 00:01:22,520
and this is a medical emergency.

26
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:24,740
The idea here is the following.

27
00:01:25,160 --> 00:01:28,020
So, imagine that if this is the lung,

28
00:01:28,660 --> 00:01:32,020
and of course the lung has a pleura attached to it,

29
00:01:32,020 --> 00:01:35,420
and now we've got the chest wall out here.

30
00:01:35,700 --> 00:01:37,480
If the patient were to get an injury,

31
00:01:37,900 --> 00:01:39,600
that would open the chest wall

32
00:01:39,600 --> 00:01:41,260
so that when the patient breathed in,

33
00:01:41,380 --> 00:01:43,040
air could come into the pleural space,

34
00:01:43,460 --> 00:01:45,860
but when they breathe out, air cannot escape,

35
00:01:46,220 --> 00:01:47,560
then every time they breathe in,

36
00:01:47,620 --> 00:01:50,120
they'd essentially be putting air into the pleural space

37
00:01:50,120 --> 00:01:52,440
as if they were pumping air into a basketball.

38
00:01:53,060 --> 00:01:54,660
So, air would continue to enter,

39
00:01:54,960 --> 00:01:57,320
which would eventually compress this lung down,

40
00:01:57,320 --> 00:01:59,180
and as it compresses this lung down,

41
00:01:59,280 --> 00:02:02,700
the pressure would also push the trachea to the opposite side.

42
00:02:03,300 --> 00:02:04,540
This is a medical emergency

43
00:02:04,540 --> 00:02:06,480
because this high tension within the thorax

44
00:02:06,480 --> 00:02:08,640
can also create pressure on the heart

45
00:02:08,640 --> 00:02:10,220
and other vital structures,

46
00:02:10,600 --> 00:02:12,680
and that's called the tension pneumothorax,

47
00:02:12,720 --> 00:02:13,720
and the way by which it's relieved

48
00:02:13,720 --> 00:02:14,920
is putting in a chest tube,

49
00:02:15,060 --> 00:02:18,700
which then allows the air to drain out from this pleural space.

50
00:02:19,180 --> 00:02:21,580
The final lesion of the pleura is called mesothelioma.

51
00:02:21,740 --> 00:02:23,880
You've heard about it previously in these discussions.

52
00:02:24,380 --> 00:02:26,820
This is a mollegate neoplasma of mesothelial cells.

53
00:02:26,820 --> 00:02:30,180
Again, highly associated with exposure to asbestos.

54
00:02:30,620 --> 00:02:32,500
It presents with recurrent pleural effusions.

55
00:02:32,820 --> 00:02:33,440
That makes sense

56
00:02:33,440 --> 00:02:35,900
because the mesothelial cells normally make fluid,

57
00:02:36,320 --> 00:02:38,060
which lubricates the pleural space,

58
00:02:38,140 --> 00:02:39,880
and so the tumor will also make fluid,

59
00:02:39,980 --> 00:02:41,500
producing recurrent pleural effusions.

60
00:02:41,980 --> 00:02:43,880
Also, there's dyspnea and chest pain,

61
00:02:44,380 --> 00:02:46,520
and then the tumor classically encases the lung

62
00:02:46,520 --> 00:02:48,180
the same way that the mesothelium,

63
00:02:48,500 --> 00:02:50,920
the normal mesothelium, normally surrounds the lung,

64
00:02:51,220 --> 00:02:52,120
and so here's a picture.

65
00:02:52,320 --> 00:02:54,120
This is the normal lung here,

66
00:02:54,460 --> 00:02:56,700
and you can see that there's this tumor

67
00:02:56,700 --> 00:02:59,740
which is massively encasing the lung,

68
00:03:00,200 --> 00:03:02,720
and this is a classic picture of a mesothelioma,

69
00:03:03,060 --> 00:03:05,380
and that concludes this section and the chapter.

